From: bigjakk <6015188+bigjakk@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 12:00:00 -0500
Subject: kcc: keep raw-input mouse motion from packets that also report a wheel tick

Stock behaviour (ui/views/win/hwnd_message_handler.cc,
HWNDMessageHandler::OnInputEvent, the WM_INPUT handler used while pointer
lock with unadjustedMovement is active): a raw mouse packet is turned into a
mouse-move event only if

  input->data.mouse.usButtonFlags != RI_MOUSE_WHEEL

so a packet whose button flags are exactly RI_MOUSE_WHEEL is skipped as a
whole, including any lLastX/lLastY it carries. A HID mouse report holds
buttons, X, Y and wheel together, so every wheel tick made while the mouse
is moving throws away one report worth of motion (1 ms at 1000 Hz). Krunker
players bind the wheel to jump or weapon switch and scroll while aiming.

What is and is not dropped by the stock test:
  - usButtonFlags == RI_MOUSE_WHEEL (vertical wheel only): dropped, with
    its motion. This is the only case this patch changes.
  - RI_MOUSE_HWHEEL only, wheel + button transition in one packet,
    button-only packets, plain moves: all forwarded already (button-only
    and h-wheel-only packets become a zero-movement move that carries the
    updated button state from UpdateRawInputButtonState). Unchanged.
  - MOUSE_MOVE_ABSOLUTE packets (RDP, VMs, tablets): forwarded, but
    set_movement() is not called for them, so they reach the renderer as
    ordinary (non raw-movement) moves at the current cursor position.
    Unchanged; an absolute wheel-only packet is still skipped.

With the feature enabled a wheel-only packet is skipped only when it has no
relative motion (lLastX == 0 && lLastY == 0, or MOUSE_MOVE_ABSOLUTE); if it
has relative motion it goes through the same code as any other move.
UpdateRawInputButtonState() is a no-op for it because no button bit is set.

Wheel handling does not change and nothing is delivered twice: OnInputEvent
never reads the wheel delta (usButtonData). Raw input is registered with
RIDEV_INPUTSINK only (no RIDEV_NOLEGACY, see
scoped_enable_unadjusted_mouse_events_win.cc), so the legacy WM_MOUSEWHEEL /
WM_MOUSEHWHEEL is still posted and is dispatched by
HandleMouseEventInternal() as a ui::MouseWheelEvent before the
"using_wm_input_" check that suppresses legacy moves. Motion is not
duplicated either, because that same check drops every legacy WM_MOUSEMOVE
while raw input is in use.

Gating: base::Feature KccRawInputKeepWheelMotion, disabled by default,
defined in the anonymous namespace of hwnd_message_handler.cc and read once.
Browser process only. With the feature off the condition is the stock one.

A/B: --enable-features=KccRawInputKeepWheelMotion. Hold pointer lock with
unadjustedMovement, move the mouse at constant speed while spinning the
wheel, and sum mousemove movementX/Y over a fixed physical distance; stock
comes up short by one report per wheel tick, the patched build does not.
Check that wheel events still arrive exactly once per tick.

Platforms: Windows only (file is Windows-only).

Not verified: nothing was compiled. The claim that real mice put motion and
a wheel tick in one RAWMOUSE packet follows from the HID report layout and
was not measured on hardware here.

diff --git a/ui/views/win/hwnd_message_handler.cc b/ui/views/win/hwnd_message_handler.cc
index 1b89cd94b56bb0f8fd4eadd14227a762bf3eaffb..b0eed3081bea1f23c23894b16825a1671af01ddb 100644
--- a/ui/views/win/hwnd_message_handler.cc
+++ b/ui/views/win/hwnd_message_handler.cc
@@ -17,6 +17,7 @@
 #include "base/auto_reset.h"
 #include "base/containers/heap_array.h"
 #include "base/debug/gdi_debug_util_win.h"
+#include "base/feature_list.h"
 #include "base/functional/bind.h"
 #include "base/functional/callback_helpers.h"
 #include "base/location.h"
@@ -306,6 +307,22 @@ constexpr int kSynthesizedMouseMessagesTimeDifference = 500;
 // True if any browser window is in a menu loop.
 bool g_is_in_native_menu_loop = false;
 
+// kcc: When enabled, a WM_INPUT mouse packet whose button flags are exactly
+// RI_MOUSE_WHEEL still delivers the relative motion it carries. Stock code
+// skips such a packet as a whole, so motion reported in the same packet as a
+// wheel tick is lost. The wheel delta itself is never read from WM_INPUT; it
+// keeps arriving through WM_MOUSEWHEEL.
+BASE_FEATURE(kKccRawInputKeepWheelMotion, base::FEATURE_DISABLED_BY_DEFAULT);
+
+// Returns true if |mouse|, a raw packet that stock code would skip as
+// wheel-only, carries relative motion that should be delivered anyway.
+bool RawWheelPacketHasMotion(const RAWMOUSE& mouse) {
+  static const bool enabled =
+      base::FeatureList::IsEnabled(kKccRawInputKeepWheelMotion);
+  return enabled && !(mouse.usFlags & MOUSE_MOVE_ABSOLUTE) &&
+         (mouse.lLastX != 0 || mouse.lLastY != 0);
+}
+
 }  // namespace
 
 class HWNDMessageHandler::ScopedWndProcDepth {
@@ -2463,7 +2480,8 @@ LRESULT HWNDMessageHandler::OnInputEvent(UINT message,
   DCHECK_EQ(size, result);
 
   if (input->header.dwType == RIM_TYPEMOUSE &&
-      input->data.mouse.usButtonFlags != RI_MOUSE_WHEEL) {
+      (input->data.mouse.usButtonFlags != RI_MOUSE_WHEEL ||
+       RawWheelPacketHasMotion(input->data.mouse))) {
     // Update the tracked button state based on any button transitions in this
     // event. This must be done before creating the MouseEvent so the event
     // reflects the correct current button state.
